R we are creating is targeting a branch that is not main. But the workflow should still run on this PR? The issue doesn't specify.\\n\\nTo be safe, we can change the pull_request trigger to not specify branches, which means it will run on all pull requests. Alternatively, we can set it to run on pull requests targeting any branch by using `branches: [ '**' ]` or simply not specifying branches.\\n\\nLet's adjust: for pull_request, we can remove the branches filter to run on all pull requests. However, the issue says \\\"on pull requests\\\", which might imply all pull requests. But the example workflow structure didn't specify.\\n\\nAlternatively, we can set it to run on pull requests targeting the main branch, which is common. Since the base branch of the PR we are creating is not main, the workflow won't run on this PR if we keep the branches filter. That would be bad because we need the CI to pass for this PR.\\n\\nTherefore, we should change the pull_request trigger to run on all branches, or at least on the branch we are targeting.\\n\\nLet's change the pull_request to:\\n\\n    pull_request:\\n      branches: [ '**' ]\\n\\nBut note: the workflow will then run on pull requests targeting any branch. That's acceptable.\\n\\nAlternatively, we can set it to run on all pull requests without specifying branches, which is equivalent to all branches.\\n\\nLet's update the workflow accordingly.\\n\\nWe'll change:\\n\\n  pull_request:\\n    branches: [ main ]\\n\\nto\\n\\n  pull_request:\\n    branches: [ '**' ]\\n\\nBut note: the `branches` filter for pull_request is optional. If we omit it, it will run on all pull requests. Let's omit it for simplicity.\\n\\nActually, the syntax: if we want to run on all pull requests, we can do:\\n\\n  pull_request:\\n\\nBut that would also trigger on pull requests from forks? We'll keep it simple.\\n\\nLet's change to:\\n\\n  pull_request:\\n\\nHowever, the YAML must be valid. We can write:\\n\\n  pull_request:\\n\\nBut then we need t